7. End-to-end workflows
Workflow A — Voice question
1. User selects a language.
2. User taps the microphone.
3. The browser requests permission if necessary.
4. User records a short question.
5. Backend validates the audio.
6. Whisper transcribes it.
7. User reviews and corrects the transcript.
8. Backend checks scope and safety routing.
9. BGE-M3 embeds the question.
10. Chroma retrieves approved, applicable passages.
11. The evidence gate checks relevance and coverage.
12. The LLM drafts a response using those passages.
13. Output checks validate citations and prohibited content.
14. The approved answer appears.
15. TTS reads the same approved answer.
16. History is saved only according to the user’s preference.
Whisper’s documented Python transcription processes audio in sliding windows; treat your initial implementation as turn-based rather than assuming native real-time partial transcription.github
Workflow B — Insufficient evidence
```
text
```
Question
   ↓
Search approved material
   ↓
No sufficient evidence
   ↓
Ask one useful clarification
OR explain that the source library cannot answer
   ↓
Offer relevant reviewed material or professional-help direction
Do not treat a high vector-similarity score as a clinical confidence score.
Workflow C — Possible urgent situation
```
text
```
Question / transcript
   ↓
Safety routing flags a possible urgent situation
   ↓
Interrupt normal information-answer flow
   ↓
Display reviewed urgent-help content
   ↓
Provide verified regional contact options
Keep the urgent-help button visible independently of this automation.
Workflow D — New document publication
```
text
```
Admin submits document
   ↓
Validate file and source
   ↓
Extract text / OCR
   ↓
Reviewer checks accuracy, scope, dates, and permissions
   ↓
Create versioned chunks
   ↓
Embed and index
   ↓
Run retrieval regression tests
   ↓
Publish
Treat document text as evidence, not as executable instructions. Retrieved text must never override your system’s safety or access-control rules.
