9. Dataset and content strategy
Use separate collections for separate purposes.
```
| Collection                | Purpose                            | Include                                           |
| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Approved health knowledge | Evidence for answers               | Reviewed official health documents                |
| Scheme knowledge          | Service-navigation answers         | Current official eligibility/application material |
| Evaluation questions      | Measure system quality             | Questions, expected answers, supporting passages  |
| Speech evaluation         | Test recognition and pronunciation | Consented recordings and verified transcripts     |
```
Common Voice offers community-created speech datasets. AI4Bharat also provides Indian-language speech resources. Use them to evaluate speech capability, not as medical evidence.ai4bharat.iitm.ac+1
Suggested starter material
* Approximately 10–20 carefully selected documents.
* Three tightly defined health topics.
* One scheme category.
* Around 50 manually checked evaluation questions.
* A separate small development set for tuning.
* Around 20–30 consented test recordings across your intended languages and recording conditions.
Include tests for:
* Questions with clear source-backed answers.
* Questions outside your library.
* Ambiguous wording.
* Potential urgent situations.
* Requests for diagnosis or dosage.
* English/Telugu mixed speech.
* Noisy recordings.
* Incorrect or incomplete transcripts.
* Prompt-injection attempts in questions and imported documents.
Kaggle and Hugging Face are discovery platforms, not guarantees of medical reliability. Verify each dataset’s origin, permitted use, relevance, and freshness before using it.kaggle+1
Do not fine-tune first. Improve document quality, retrieval, prompts, and evaluation before adding training work.
10. Privacy, deployment, and cost boundaries
For the prototype, I recommend these defaults:
* Do not retain raw audio after transcription.
* Do not store conversations unless the user opts in.
* Keep personal conversations out of the shared retrieval index.
* Use synthetic conversations during demonstrations.
* Keep provider keys on the backend.
* Remove sensitive content from application logs.
* Restrict source uploads to authorized staff.
* Encrypt transport and stored sensitive data.
* Document deletion and backup-retention behavior.
* Review privacy, security, and applicable legal requirements before handling real patient information.
Google AI Studio provides access to Gemini API development, while Ollama offers local and cloud model execution. These are alternative deployment options; neither automatically makes your overall system suitable for sensitive healthcare data. Verify the exact service terms and data-handling configuration before sending such information.aistudio.google+1
Also, “open-source” does not mean “free to operate.” Your budget still needs to consider hosting, inference hardware, storage, API usage, and maintenance. Check the selected model and voice licenses individually.
A practical development deployment is:
```
text
```
Web frontend
      ↓
FastAPI backend
      ├── PostgreSQL
      ├── Chroma
      ├── Speech/model worker
      ├── Ingestion worker
      └── Private file storage
Add real-time Pipecat orchestration only after this path works reliably.
11. Implementation order
```
| Stage | Build                                        | Exit condition                                          |
| ----- | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1     | Scope, wireframes, language decision         | Clear supported and unsupported use cases               |
| 2     | Document ingestion and text-only retrieval   | Correct source passages appear for test questions       |
| 3     | Grounded text answers and abstention         | Unsupported questions do not receive invented answers   |
| 4     | Voice input and transcript correction        | Users can reliably correct recognition mistakes         |
| 5     | Speech output and playback                   | Audio matches approved text                             |
| 6     | Login, optional history, privacy settings    | Ownership and deletion tests pass                       |
| 7     | Admin approval and review queue              | Unapproved content cannot be retrieved                  |
| 8     | Telugu evaluation and accessibility testing  | Language-specific failures are understood and addressed |
| 9     | Performance, failure handling, demonstration | End-to-end flow works under realistic conditions        |
```
Track at least:
* Retrieval success against expected passages.
* Answer support and citation correctness.
* Appropriate abstention.
* Speech recognition errors.
* Telugu pronunciation and translation quality.
* Time to first visible answer.
* Time to first spoken audio.
* Failure recovery.
Have a qualified healthcare reviewer assess medical content and safety behavior before presenting the product as ready for real healthcare use.